present  werd really it's more of a statement  that i want to make and that is about a  proposal that we have put forward and i  hope will be taken forward within the  next couple of months now I what I just  preface it by saying that I can't  imagine that there's a single person in  this room who would not agree that the  destruction of a people's cultural  heritage on the scale we've recently  seen in Iraq under the consequent  attempt to deprive that people the  legacy of its past and hence its  identity represents an act of  unspeakable inhumanity and yet  fortunately it simply isn't possible to  totally eradicate the ancient past no  matter how severe the attempt something  always remains and it is after all  that's something that those of us who  are archaeologists work with all the  time using the debris of the ancient  abandonments and destructions to  reconstruct civilizations and history  and so it is that whilst we can't do  anything to prevent what's happening in  those parts of Iraq and Syria controlled  by the so-called Islamic stage we can at  least prepare for the time when it will  be possible once again to access these  areas and yes as John Curtis has rightly  said huge resources are going to be  needed both in terms of manpower and  money when that time arises we can't  predict whether we're talking about one  year into the future two years five  years even ten years but what I do  believe is we have to do something now  and so what we are going to do at the  British Museum is to create specialized  training program leading to the creation  if you like of a specialized task  horse-based initially at the BM and  subsequently in the field in Iraq this  would be a task force designed or  trained if you like in all the skills of  emergency heritage management  specifically what we're going to do very  soon we hope is to recruit two  archeologists experience archaeologists  who have worked in Iraq for a period of  five years in the first instance they  would Institute a six month training  program at the BM to which for heritage  sector Iraqi professionals at a time  would be invited and we'll work closely  obviously with the Iraqi authorities to  choose the right people the program will  be devoted to museum and site disaster  work construction and manipulation of  databases digitization of objects  documents emergency retrieval strategies  forensic collection and documentation  methodologies extensive multi-purpose  photographic training principles  conservation architectural restoration  techniques of recording and data  representation systems and so on and so  forth an intensive course following the  six-month training period these BM  curators or archaeologists would return  to Iraq with the trainees in order to  set up programs within their museums or  institutions using appropriate computer  and photographic equipment to revive the  documentation of existing collections  and develop strategies to cope with a  very diverse requirements of  archaeological site management a core  element within this second six-month  period will be the detailed instruction  in techniques of rescue archaeology  involving the establishment of joint  training excavations so the effect of  this rolling programme which as I say we  want to start always straight away will  be to create a large and well trained  Corps that can cope with a full range of  archaeological heritage needs when it  becomes possible  and to survey the archaeological  landscape of Iraq in whatever parts  become accessible this will mean that  local heritage professionals we fully  qualified to respond to the realities of  recent damage and establish priorities  for action and best practice thank you

